\details{
Genotypes are coded \code{-1}/\code{0}/\code{1}. Tetraploid wheat lines are largely
homozygous (about 8\% heterozygous), so the dataset suits additive
schemes (cf. \link{SoySNP50K_div}).

\strong{Polyploidy note.} Durum/emmer wheat is an \emph{allotetraploid}
(2n = 4x = 28, AABB) with \strong{disomic} inheritance: homologues pair
within a subgenome, and homoeologues do not (under the \emph{Ph1} system). A
subgenome-specific SNP on chromosome 1A therefore segregates as an
ordinary diploid locus linked only to other 1A markers, which is exactly
what coding each subgenome-specific 90K SNP as a diploid locus (\code{-1}/
\code{0}/\code{1}, from the deposited VCF's \code{0/0}, \code{0/1}, \code{1/1} calls) with one
genetic map per chromosome 1A..7B represents: the diploid-per-subgenome
model captures the inheritance correctly. The only approximation is at
the assay level: the 90K array's probes are subgenome-specific, so a
marker that happened to co-amplify both homoeologues would need a
composite 0..4 dosage call, which is a data-curation matter rather than
a meiosis-model limitation.

The genetic-map column \code{cm} is a synthetic map built from physical
positions with \code{simplePHENOTYPES::synthetic_map()}, one map per
chromosome.
